{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"horizontal scaling horizontal scaling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md","fileName":"Horizontal Scaling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Horizontal Scaling","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 22:12\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Cloud Scalability and Resilience Patterns]] [[Software Quality Attributes and Architecture Tradeoffs]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Scalability and Resilience Patterns","Software Quality Attributes and Architecture Tradeoffs","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf","hands-onsoftwarearchitecturewithc8andnetcore3.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"horizontal scaling horizontal scaling","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Horizontal Scaling.md","fileName":"Horizontal Scaling.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Horizontal Scaling","heading":"Horizontal Scaling","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Horizontal scaling changes the number of application instances rather than the size of one instance. A load balancer distributes requests among several servers, increasing aggregate capacity and allowing one node to fail without necessarily ending the service. The application must handle session state and shared data deliberately; requests tied to one server through sticky sessions limit how evenly load can be spread.\n\nCloud platforms can add replicas dynamically, but the architecture must remove or externalize instance-local state and ensure downstream databases or services can absorb the increased concurrency.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Scal